          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Atoms are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+directed+complex"} regular directed complexes
            with a maximal cell. With the correct notion of maps, they are a contender for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shape+for+higher+structures"} geometric shape for higher categories
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (atom)
              %br/
              An
              %strong atom
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+directed+complex"} regular directed complex
              whose underlying poset has a maximal element.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+directed+complex"} regular directed complex
            %strong morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi P
                  %mo →
                  %mi Q
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon P \to Q
            between regular directed complex as a function of the underlying posets that respects both input and output faces, that is,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            induces a bijection between
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Δ
                    %mi α
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^\alpha x
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi Δ
                      %mi α
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Delta^\alpha f(x)
          %p
            This notion is, albeit well-suited for defining regular directed complexes, quite restrictive. For instance, the point
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                    %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
            is not a terminal object in this category. In fact, if there is a morphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon P \to Q
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi dim
                    %mi P
                    %mo ≤
                    %mi dim
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \dim P \le \dim Q
          %p Instead, we define a less restrictive notion of map, that strictly generalizes the morphisms.
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p In the following definition, we will impose two conditions (finality, and fibration)
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (cartesian map of regular directed complex)
              %br/
              A
              %strong cartesian map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi P
                    %mo →
                    %mi Q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon P \to Q
              between regular directed complexes is a function of the underlying set such that
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                is a
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} Grothendieck fibration
                (in particular,
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
                is order-preserving);
              %li
                for all
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in P
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi α
                        %mo ∈
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo ,
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha \in \{ -, + \}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
                .maruku-equation
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msubsup
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi n
                          %mi α
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo =
                        %msubsup
                          %mo ∂
                          %mi n
                          %mi α
                        %mi f
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi x
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mo ;
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                        f(\partial^\alpha_n x) = \partial^\alpha_n f(x);
              %li
                for all
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi P
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in P
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %msubsup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi n
                        %mi α
                      %mi x
                      %mo →
                      %msubsup
                        %mo ∂
                        %mi n
                        %mi α
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f \colon \partial^\alpha_n x \to \partial^\alpha_n f(x)
                is
                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functor"} final
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              In the previous definition, the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+fibration"} fibration
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/final+functor"} finality
              condition implicitely see the underlying posets as categories.
          %p
            This new definition of cartesian map generalizes the notion of morphism in the following sense:
          .num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p Any morphism of regular directed complex is a cartesian map, and a cartesian map is a morphism if and only if it is dimension-preserving.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (atom category)
              %br/
              We define the
              = succeed "," do
                %strong atom category
              = succeed "," do
                %strong
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊙
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeleton
              of the category of atoms and cartesian maps.
              %br/
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
              is a strict
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+Reedy+category"} Cisinski generalized Reedy category
              (Def 2.4), where
            %ul
              %li
                the degree map
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mo →
                      %mi ℕ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \colon \odot \to \mathbb{N}
                is given by the dimension
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo ↦
                        %mi dim
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto \dim U
              %li
                the wide subcategory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo ⊙
                        %mo +
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot^+
                is the cartesian maps that are injective;
              %li
                the wide subcategory
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mo ⊙
                        %mo −
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot^-
                is the cartesian maps that are surjective;
            %p
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              We use the prefix “strict” in the previous definition to mean that the factorisation system
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mo −
                    %mo ,
                    %msup
                      %mo ⊙
                      %mo +
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\odot^-, \odot^+)
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+factorization+system"} strict
              rather than merely
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthogonal+factorization+system"} orthogonal
          %p
            In particular, any cartesian map of atom factors uniquely as a surjection, which we also call a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong collapse
            = succeed "," do
              %strong inclusion
          %p
            The inclusions are very-well behaved, we have
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              be an atom, there is a one-to-one correspondance between elements of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
              and inclusions
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \hookrightarrow U
              given by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                    %mo ↦
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ι
                      %mi x
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} cl
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    x \in U \mapsto (\iota_x \colon \mathrm{cl}(x) \hookrightarrow U)
            %p
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %mi V
                    %mo ↪
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota \colon V \hookrightarrow U
              is an inclusion, there is an isomorphisms
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi ι
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \cong \iota(V) \subseteq U
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is an atom, so is
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ι
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota(V)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in U
              which uniquely characterize
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ι
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \iota
              (since the category is skeletal).
              %br/
          %p
            However, the precise behavior of collapses is not as explicit as in the case of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex"} simplex
            or
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+cubes"} cube
            categories. Nevertheless, we have
          .num_lemma
            %h6 Lemma
            %p
              The point
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
              is the terminal object of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊙
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
          as well as the Cisinski-Eilenberg-Zilber axiom which state that
          %ul
            %li every degeneracy has a section;
            %li if two degeneracies have the same set of section, they are equal.
          %h3#stability_of_operations Stability of operations
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+directed+complex#operations"} Gray product, the join, and the suspension
              are again atoms. Therefore:
              %br/
          %p
          .num_prop
            %h6 Proposition
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo ⊙
                    %mo ,
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mo ,
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\odot, \otimes, \mathbf{1})
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
          %p
            Notice that the only reason why the join
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋆
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \star
            does not make a monoidal category is because its unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ∅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \emptyset
            is only a regular directed complex, but not an atom.
          %p
          .num_defn
            %h6 Definition
            %p
              %strong (oriented simplex)
              %br/
              The
              %strong
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                \-th oriented simplex
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\Delta}_n
              is defined inductively by
            %ul
              %li
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi Δ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ≔
                      %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                        %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\Delta}_0 \coloneqq \mathbf{1}
                ;
              %li
                inductively,
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                          %mi n
                        %mo ≔
                        %msub
                          %mover
                            %mi Δ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                          %mrow
                            %mi n
                            %mo −
                            %mn 1
                        %mo ⋆
                        %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                          %mn 1
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\Delta}_n \coloneqq \vec{\Delta}_{n - 1} \star \mathbf{1}
            %p
              We call
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\Delta}
              the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/full+subcategory"} full subcategory
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
              on oriented simplices.
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi Δ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vec{\Delta}
              is isomorphic to the
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi d
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d_i \colon \vec{\Delta}_{n - 1} \to \vec{\Delta}_{n}
              can be reconstructed as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo !
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi ∅
                    %mo ⋆
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ↪
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⋆
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathrm{id} \star ! \star \mathrm{id} \colon \vec{\Delta}_{i - 1} \star \emptyset \star \vec{\Delta}_{n - i - 1} \hookrightarrow \vec{\Delta}_{i - 1} \star \mathbf{1} \star \vec{\Delta}_{n - i - 1}
            %p
              and the codegeneracy
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi s
                      %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s_i \colon \vec{\Delta}_{n + 1} \to \vec{\Delta}_{n}
              can be reconstructed as
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mo !
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⋆
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⋆
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ↪
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo ⋆
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ⋆
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi Δ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo −
                        %mi i
                        %mo −
                        %mn 1
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\mathrm{id} \star ! \star \mathrm{id} \colon \vec{\Delta}_{i - 1} \star \vec{\Delta}_1 \star \vec{\Delta}_{n - i - 1} \hookrightarrow \vec{\Delta}_{i - 1} \star \mathbf{1} \star \vec{\Delta}_{n - i - 1}.
            %p
          %h3#test_category Test category
          %p
          .num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+category"} test category
          .proof
            %h6 Proof
            %p
              As
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{1}
              is the terminal object,
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
              is aspherical. Therefore, it suffices to prove that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
              is a local test category. This follows from
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "#Cisinski06"} Cisinski06, Corollaire 8.2.16
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cylinder+object"} cylinder object
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                      %mo ↦
                      %mover
                        %mi I
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} →
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \mapsto \vec{I} \otimes U
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
              on presheaves. Since atoms are stable under Gray products, the map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi U
                    %mo ↠
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \otimes U \twoheadrightarrow U
              is representable, so is a weak equivalence (in the sense of
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/test+category"} here
          %p
            In fact,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
            is even a strict test category.
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              A presheaf over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊙
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \odot
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/diagrammatic+set"} diagrammatic set
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplex+category"} simplex category
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/regular+directed+complex"} regular directed complex
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+shape+for+higher+structures"} geometric shape for higher categories
          %h2#references References
          %p For the basic properties of atoms, and their relations to cube, simplices, and other shapes:
          %ul
            %li#Hadzihasanovic2024
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Amar+Hadzihasanovic"}> Amar Hadzihasanovic
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Combinatorics of higher-categorical diagrams
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.07273"} link
          %p Statements about Eilenberg-Zilber category and test category are proved here:
          %ul
            %li#ChanavatHadzihasanovic2024
              Clémence Chanavat, Amar Hadzihasanovic,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Diagrammatic sets as a model of homotopy types
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2407.06285v1"} arXiv:2407.06285
          %p In part 8, we find a lot of results to prove that Eilenberg-Zilber related categories are test categories:
          %ul
            %li#Cisinski06
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Denis-Charles+Cisinski"}> Denis-Charles Cisinski
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Les préfaisceaux comme modèles des types d’homotopie
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://smf.emath.fr/publications/les-prefaisceaux-comme-modeles-des-types-dhomotopie"} doi:10.24033/ast.715
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/AST_2006__308__R1_0/"} NUMDAM
        .revisedby
